Found from kubernetes-ocp k290 (2026-10-31, live on a 1.36 cluster).

A client that does not ask for strict field validation gets unknown fields dropped by the API server. The server only sends a `Warning:` response header (299 "unknown field ..."), and Kubernetes::REST shows the caller nothing. In kubernetes-ocp, an OCPNode written against a CRD whose schema predated spec.nodeIP was stored without that field. The node then joined on the wrong address, and nothing said so.

Ask (either one closes the silent path; both would be best):
1. A way to send `fieldValidation=Strict`, per request (create/update/patch/apply) and/or as a client default. The server then refuses unknown or duplicate fields with a 4xx, and the caller sees it as an error. kubectl does this by default (`--validate=strict`).
2. Surface the response `Warning` headers: return them to the caller, or log them through a hook the caller can set. That way a dropped field is at least visible.

Done = the option(s) and their POD, plus a test with a fake transport: a request carries the fieldValidation query parameter, and a Warning header reaches the caller.

Hub decision (perl-developer-bb, 2026-11-06), not a maintainer decision: both asks. (1) fieldValidation can be set per request (create, update, patch, apply) and as a client default (Strict, Warn, Ignore); without either the client does not send the parameter, as today. (2) Warning headers of the response reach the caller through a callback set on the client; without a callback nothing changes on STDERR. Tests with a fake transport: the request carries the query parameter, and a Warning header reaches the callback.
Coordinator's choices (mine): this client has no server-side apply (patch types are strategic, merge, json), so 'apply' has no method to go on; the per-request option goes on create, update, patch and on the two status writes patch_status and update_status, and the client default reaches all five (and ensure() through them). Per-request option fieldValidation (the query parameter's name, as delete's propagationPolicy and list's limit/continue/resourceVersion); client attribute field_validation (snake_case like every attribute here); per request wins. Another value croaks before anything is sent, naming the option and the allowed values, not the value. Callback attribute on_warning, called once per Warning header value with ($text, $context): $text the warning text (the quoted part of '299 - "..."', else the raw value), $context the string an APIError carries; on every response that passes check_response, so async clients on the seam get it too, error responses included. Kubernetes::REST::HTTPResponse gains an optional headers attribute (lowercased names, arrayref of values); LWPIO and HTTPTinyIO fill it; a response object without headers means no warnings, no error.

